Notification dispatch and session ownership
===========================================

The shared alert/SLA notification helper sends network recipients sequentially.
Provider policy checks and delivery tracking share the caller's AsyncSession;
that session is one mutable transaction and cannot safely flush concurrently.
Previously, multi-recipient batches could lose returned delivery identities
while logging tracking failures, and failed attempts without IDs were not retried.

A provider exception remains a failed result and does not prevent the helper
from attempting later recipients. Cancellation propagates immediately: recipients
not yet started remain unsent, and the helper does not publish batch retries.
Earlier external sends may already have occurred. Network recipient order follows
the configured channel and recipient order. Slow providers therefore add to batch
latency; this correction does not establish a throughput or latency guarantee.

For network delivery, the helper does not commit or roll back the caller's
transaction. Returned delivery IDs describe flushed rows, not committed records
or final recipient acceptance. The existing in-app path still explicitly commits;
its transaction ownership is not changed by this correction.

Retry publication still occurs before the caller's eventual commit. A rollback,
broker outage, or process crash can leave missing work or externally delivered
messages without durable tracking. A transactional outbox and end-to-end provider
idempotency remain separate work. No exactly-once or automatic crash recovery
claim is made. Never rerun a whole batch blindly after uncertain delivery.

Unit guards cover shared-session exclusivity, identity association, ordinary
provider errors and cancellation. Qualification additionally uses actual mapped
asynchronous database operations with synthetic providers; it does not contact
live mail, messaging, SMS, or customer hardware endpoints.
